---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import TableOfContents from '../../components/sections/TableOfContents.astro';

const toc = [
	{ id: 'how-they-work', label: '1. How They Work: A Quick Comparison' },
	{ id: 'head-to-head-gate-vs-butterfly', label: '2. Head-to-Head: Gate vs Butterfly' },
	{ id: 'where-each-valve-belongs', label: '3. Where Each Valve Belongs in Fire Protection Systems' },
	{ id: 'signal-valves-dn80', label: '4. Signal Valves: The DN80 Specialists' },
	{ id: 'purchasing-considerations', label: '5. Purchasing Considerations for B2B Buyers' },
	{ id: 'recommendation-summary', label: '6. Recommendation Summary' },
	{ id: 'faq', label: 'Frequently Asked Questions' }
];

const faq = [
	{
		question: 'Should I use a gate valve or a butterfly valve on a fire main?',
		answer: 'For fire mains that require full, unobstructed flow with minimal pressure loss, a resilient seat gate valve (such as the Z41X) is the conventional choice for isolation duty. Butterfly valves (such as the D71X) are preferred where space is tight, where quarter-turn operation is needed, or where weight matters — for example in valve manifolds and sprinkler branch lines. Many projects use both: gate valves for main isolation and butterfly valves for branch control.'
	},
	{
		question: 'Which is cheaper, a gate valve or a butterfly valve?',
		answer: 'At the same nominal size and pressure class, wafer-type butterfly valves are generally 20–40% cheaper than flanged gate valves because they are lighter, use less material and have a simpler body. However, the total installed cost also depends on flanges, actuators and space. For large diameters (DN200 and above) the butterfly valve cost advantage grows significantly.'
	},
	{
		question: 'What is the purpose of a signal butterfly valve in a fire sprinkler system?',
		answer: 'A signal valve (such as the DN80 grooved wafer butterfly valve) is fitted with a tamper/limit switch that sends an electrical signal to the fire alarm panel whenever the valve is closed or partially closed. This ensures the sprinkler system is never left isolated unintentionally — a mandatory safety requirement in NFPA-13-style installations.'
	},
	{
		question: 'What is the pressure rating difference between Z41X and D71X valves?',
		answer: 'The ductile iron Z41X resilient seat gate valve and the D71X wafer butterfly valve are both commonly supplied at PN16 (1.6 MPa), with PN10 variants available. For fire protection duty, PN16 covers standard municipal and industrial systems. Always confirm the maximum static pressure of your network and specify PN25 if required.'
	}
];

const valveProducts = [
	{
		slug: 'resilient-seat-flanged-gate-valve-z41x',
		name: 'Ductile Iron Resilient Seat Flanged Gate Valve (Z41X)',
		img: '/assets/products/resilient-seat-flanged-gate-valve-z41x.jpg',
		desc: 'Full-bore isolation valve with bubble-tight seal for fire mains'
	},
	{
		slug: 'wafer-type-butterfly-valve-d71x',
		name: 'Ductile Iron Rubber Lined Wafer Butterfly Valve (D71X)',
		img: '/assets/products/wafer-type-butterfly-valve-d71x.jpg',
		desc: 'Compact quarter-turn valve for tight spaces and branch lines'
	},
	{
		slug: 'signal-grooved-wafer-butterfly-valve-dn80',
		name: 'Signal Grooved Wafer Butterfly Valve (DN80-PN16)',
		img: '/assets/products/signal-grooved-wafer-butterfly-valve-dn80.jpg',
		desc: 'Grooved signal valve with tamper switch for sprinkler systems'
	}
];
---

<Layout
	title="Gate Valve vs Butterfly Valve: Which One for Fire Protection Systems?"
	description="Gate vs butterfly valve for fire protection: flow, pressure drop, cost. Z41X gate, D71X butterfly, DN80 signal valve — selection guidance for B2B buyers."
	type="article"
	faq={faq}
>
	<main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14 mb-16">
		<!-- Breadcrumbs -->
		<nav class="text-xs text-gray-400 font-bold uppercase tracking-widest mb-6 flex gap-2">
			<a href="/" class="hover:text-blue-600">Home</a>
			<span>/</span>
			<span class="text-gray-600">Guides</span>
		</nav>

		<article class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm border border-gray-100 p-6 sm:p-10">
			<p class="text-brand-red font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-sm mb-3">Industrial Valve Guide</p>
			<h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 leading-tight tracking-tight">Gate Valve vs Butterfly Valve: Which One for Fire Protection Systems?</h1>
			<p class="mt-5 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed text-lg">It is one of the most common questions in fire protection procurement: gate valve or butterfly valve? The honest answer is that they are not interchangeable — they are complementary. Each has a zone where it is clearly the right choice, and most properly engineered fire systems use both. This guide gives you a decision framework based on flow, space, speed of operation and cost, with reference to the actual valve configurations used in fire mains and sprinkler systems.</p>

			<!-- Product strip -->
			<div class="mt-8 grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
				{valveProducts.map(p => (
					<a href={`/products/${p.slug}/`} class="group bg-gray-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden hover:border-brand-red hover:shadow-md transition">
						<img src={p.img} alt={p.name} loading="lazy" class="w-full h-32 object-contain bg-white p-2" />
						<div class="p-3">
							<p class="text-xs font-bold text-gray-800 group-hover:text-brand-red leading-snug transition-colors">{p.name}</p>
							<p class="text-[10px] text-gray-400 mt-1 line-clamp-2">{p.desc}</p>
						</div>
					</a>
				))}
			</div>

			
			<TableOfContents headings={toc} />

			<h2 id="how-they-work" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">1. How They Work: A Quick Comparison</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">A <strong>gate valve</strong> uses a wedge that slides perpendicular to the flow path. Fully open, the wedge is completely out of the waterway, leaving a full-bore, straight-through opening — which is why gate valves have negligible pressure loss. A <strong>butterfly valve</strong> uses a rotating disc on a central shaft. The disc stays in the waterway even when open, creating a small but measurable pressure drop, but the valve operates with a simple quarter turn.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">This one mechanical difference drives almost every practical difference between them.</p>

			<h2 id="head-to-head-gate-vs-butterfly" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">2. Head-to-Head: Gate vs Butterfly</h2>
			<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200 my-5">
				<table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 text-sm">
					<thead class="bg-gray-50">
						<tr>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">Criteria</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">Gate Valve (Z41X)</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">Butterfly Valve (D71X)</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody class="bg-white divide-y divide-gray-200">
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Flow characteristics</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Full bore, near-zero pressure loss</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Disc in flow path, small pressure loss</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Operation speed</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Multi-turn (slower, especially large sizes)</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Quarter-turn (fast, easy to actuate)</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Space &amp; weight</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Long face-to-face, heavy at large DN</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Compact wafer body, up to 70% lighter</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Cost (same DN/PN)</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Higher material &amp; machining cost</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Typically 20–40% lower</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Sealing</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Bubble-tight with resilient seat</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Rubber-lined, tight at rated pressure</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Best duty</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Main isolation, full-flow open/closed</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Branch control, throttling, tight spaces</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>

			<h2 id="where-each-valve-belongs" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">3. Where Each Valve Belongs in Fire Protection Systems</h2>
			<h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-800 mt-6 mb-3">Gate valves for main isolation</h3>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">On fire mains and pump discharge lines, the priority is delivering maximum flow with minimum friction loss. A <a href="/products/resilient-seat-flanged-gate-valve-z41x/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">ductile iron Z41X resilient seat gate valve</a> is the standard isolation valve here: full bore, bubble-tight closure, and a flat unobstructed waterway when open. Because these valves operate rarely (open/closed only), the slower multi-turn operation is not a practical drawback.</p>
			<h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-800 mt-6 mb-3">Butterfly valves for branch lines and tight spaces</h3>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Where valves are packed into sprinkler riser rooms, plant rooms or valve manifolds, space is money. The <a href="/products/wafer-type-butterfly-valve-d71x/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">D71X wafer butterfly valve</a> installs between two flanges with a fraction of the face-to-face length and weight of a gate valve, and its quarter-turn lever or worm gear makes operation and actuator mounting straightforward. This makes it the default choice for sprinkler branch control and section isolation.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">For a complete breakdown of sprinkler-system valves — wet alarm, deluge and pre-action — see our <a href="/guides/fire-sprinkler-system-valves-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">fire sprinkler system valves guide</a>.</p>

			<h2 id="signal-valves-dn80" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">4. Signal Valves: The DN80 Specialists</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Sprinkler systems under NFPA-13-style codes require a monitored isolation valve: if someone closes the supply valve, the fire alarm panel must know immediately. The <a href="/products/signal-grooved-wafer-butterfly-valve-dn80/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">DN80 signal grooved wafer butterfly valve</a> integrates a tamper/limit switch with a grooved connection, so it bolts into the piping with couplings, reports its open/closed status to the panel, and still gives you the space and cost benefits of a butterfly design. This is the valve you specify when the drawing says "monitored OS&amp;Y or butterfly valve".</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Still deciding between wet, dry or pre-action system types? Our <a href="/guides/fire-sprinkler-system-wet-dry-preaction/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">wet, dry and pre-action sprinkler comparison</a> explains which system fits which building risk profile.</p>

			<h2 id="purchasing-considerations" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">5. Purchasing Considerations for B2B Buyers</h2>
			<ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2 mb-4">
				<li><strong>Specify body material and coating</strong> — ductile iron QT450 with epoxy coating resists corrosion in wet fire systems; ask for the coating spec in the quotation.</li>
				<li><strong>Match the pressure class to your network</strong> — PN16 is standard; confirm static pressure before ordering, PN25 for high-rise zones.</li>
				<li><strong>Connection style matters</strong> — flanged for gate valves, wafer or grooved for butterfly; confirm against your pipe schedule (grooved couplings save installation time).</li>
				<li><strong>Signal valves must list the switch spec</strong> — ask for the limit switch type and IP rating to interface with your alarm panel.</li>
				<li><strong>Request test reports</strong> — hydrostatic and seat leakage test certificates for the actual batch, not a generic template.</li>
				<li><strong>Consolidate your order</strong> — ordering gate, butterfly and signal valves from one certified manufacturer simplifies certification documentation and spares management. Run our <a href="/guides/b2b-valve-sourcing-checklist/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">B2B valve sourcing checklist</a> before committing to a supplier.</li>
			</ul>

			<h2 id="recommendation-summary" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">6. Recommendation Summary</h2>
			<div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 p-6 my-5">
				<ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
					<li><strong>Fire mains / pump discharge → gate valve (Z41X)</strong>: full flow, minimal loss, reliable isolation.</li>
					<li><strong>Branch lines / riser rooms / manifolds → butterfly valve (D71X)</strong>: compact, light, fast operation.</li>
					<li><strong>Monitored isolation in sprinkler systems → signal valve (DN80)</strong>: tamper switch integrated, grooved for fast install.</li>
					<li>When in doubt, confirm with your system designer — most projects need all three.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>

			<!-- FAQ Section -->
			<h2 id="faq" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-6 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
			<div class="space-y-4">
				{faq.map(item => (
					<details class="group bg-gray-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 p-5 open:border-brand-red/30">
						<summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-gray-800 cursor-pointer list-none">
							<span>{item.question}</span>
							<span class="text-brand-blue text-xl transition-transform group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
						</summary>
						<p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed text-sm">{item.answer}</p>
					</details>
				))}
			</div>

			<!-- CTA -->
			<div class="mt-12 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
				<div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-brand-blue to-blue-800 px-6 sm:px-10 py-10 text-center">
					<h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white tracking-tight">Building a Fire System Valve Package?</h2>
					<p class="mt-3 text-blue-100 max-w-xl mx-auto text-sm leading-relaxed">Tell us your pipe schedule and pressure class — we quote gate, butterfly and signal valves as one certified package within 12 hours.</p>
					<div class="mt-6 flex flex-col sm:flex-row justify-center gap-4">
						<a href="/contact/?product=Valve%20Selection" class="px-8 py-4 bg-brand-red text-white font-bold rounded-lg hover:opacity-90 transition">Request a Quote</a>
						<a href="https://wa.me/8618684324501?text=Hi%2C%20I%20need%20help%20selecting%20valves%20for%20a%20fire%20system" class="px-8 py-4 bg-white text-brand-blue font-bold rounded-lg hover:bg-blue-50 transition" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chat on WhatsApp</a>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</article>
	</main>
</Layout>
